[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a step-by-step recipe you can copy-paste into a MapLibre GL JS app.  
It creates a **raster-DEM source**, adds it to the style, and then draws a **hillshade layer** that visualises the terrain.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 1.0** (or any recent 2.x/3.x build) | Hillshade is a built-in layer type. |
| A **raster-DEM** tile service (e.g. Mapbox Terrain-RGB, Open-Topo-Map DEM, your own MBTiles server, etc.) | The source supplies elevation data that the hillshade shader reads. |
| CORS / token access if the DEM tiles are behind authentication | The browser must be able to fetch the tiles. |
| (Optional) **Terrain** enabled on the map – not required for hillshade, but often useful if you also want 3-D terrain. | `map.setTerrain({source: 'dem'})` |

---

## 2. Add the DEM source to the style

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the map (any style you like)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // HTML element id
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [-122.447, 37.753],     // initial view
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ When the style is ready, add the raster-DEM source
map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.addSource('dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    // Example: Mapbox Terrain-RGB (replace with your own URL/template)
    //   https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.pngraw?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN
    //   (or any XYZ/WMTS endpoint that returns 256-px PNG/TIFF DEM tiles)
    tiles: [
      'https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.pngraw?access_token=YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN'
    ],
    tileSize: 256,          // most DEM services use 256 px tiles
    maxzoom: 15,            // adjust to what your source supports
    // If your DEM tiles are encoded as Mapbox Terrain-RGB, set this flag:
    // (MapLibre will decode the RGB → elevation automatically)
    //   "encoding": "mapbox"
    // For plain 16-bit grayscale DEMs you can omit it.
  });
```

> **Tip:** If you are serving your own DEM tiles, make sure the URL template ends with `{z}/{x}/{y}` (or `{z}/{x}/{y}.{ext}`) and that the server sends the correct `Content-Type` (`image/png`, `image/jpeg`, `image/tiff`, …).  

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

```js
  // 3️⃣ Add a hillshade layer that reads from the DEM source
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'hillshade',
    type: 'hillshade',
    source: 'dem',
    // Optional: limit the layer to a specific zoom range
    minzoom: 0,
    maxzoom: 15,

    // Paint properties – tweak to taste
    paint: {
      // Light direction (azimuth, degrees clockwise from north)
      'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,

      // How strong the shading looks (default 0.5)
      'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,

      // Colors for the three shading components
      'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',   // dark side
      'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)', // lit side
      'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.2)' // mid-tone accent
    }
  });
});
```

### What the paint properties do

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | 0-360° | Sun azimuth; 315° = north-west (common default). |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | 0-1 (or higher) | Makes relief look steeper; 0 = flat, 1 = full relief. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | any CSS color | Dark side of slopes. |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | any CSS color | Bright side of slopes. |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | any CSS color | Mid-tone “ambient” shading. |

You can animate the sun direction, change the exaggeration with a UI slider, or switch the colors for a night-mode look – just call `map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', …)`.

---

## 4. (Optional) Enable 3-D terrain as well

If you also want the map surface to rise according to the same DEM, add terrain:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

> **Note:** Hillshade works **without** terrain, but when both are present the hillshade stays on the ground while the terrain mesh lifts the surface. This is the usual “shaded relief” look.

---

## 5. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447, 37.753],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    map.addSource('dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      tiles: [
        // Replace with your own DEM endpoint or token
        'https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.pngraw?access_token=YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 15
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',
        'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.2)'
      }
    });

    // Optional terrain
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or serve it via a local web server) and you should see a shaded-relief map over the base style.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears (just the base map)** | DEM source not loading (404, CORS, wrong token) | Open dev-tools → Network → verify tile requests succeed. Add proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers or use a token that has `tiles` scope. |
| **Hillshade looks flat / no contrast** | `hillshade-exaggeration` is too low or DEM elevation range is tiny | Increase `hillshade-exaggeration` (e.g., `1.0`) or use a higher-resolution DEM. |
| **Colors look wrong (all white or all black)** | Paint colors set to fully opaque black/white, or the DEM encoding is mismatched | Use semi-transparent RGBA values as in the example, and ensure the DEM is correctly decoded (`encoding: "mapbox"` for Terrain-RGB). |
| **Map freezes when zooming fast** | Too many DEM tiles being requested (high `maxzoom` on a low-performance server) | Lower `maxzoom` on the source, or cache tiles via a CDN. |
| **Hillshade disappears after style change** | The new style overwrites the source/layer | Re-add the source/layer after each `style.load` event, or use `map.setStyle(..., { diff: true })` and keep the source in a separate style JSON. |

---

### TL;DR checklist

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (`type: 'raster-dem'`, `tiles: [...]`, `tileSize`, `maxzoom`).  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** (`type: 'hillshade'`, `source: '<your-source-id>'`).  
3. **Configure paint** (`hillshade-illumination-direction`, `hillshade-exaggeration`, colors).  
4. (Optional) **Enable terrain** with `map.setTerrain({source: '<id>'})`.  
5. Verify tile access (CORS, token) and adjust paint values to get the visual effect you want.

That’s all you need to get a hillshade layer rendering from a raster-DEM in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

Below is a step-by-step recipe you can copy-paste into a typical MapLibre GL JS app.  
It creates a **raster-DEM source**, adds it to the map, and then draws a **hillshade layer** on top of whatever base map you already have.

---

## 1. Make sure you’re using a recent MapLibre GL JS version  

Hillshade support landed in the 1.x line and is stable in all 2.x releases.  
```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
```

---

## 2. Add a raster-DEM source  

The source must be of type **`raster-dem`** (not `raster`).  
You can point it at any XYZ/WMTS tile service that returns 16-bit elevation tiles (GeoTIFF, PNG, etc.).  
If you have a local MBTiles file, serve it with a simple tile server (e.g. `tileserver-gl`, `tileserver-php`, `tessera`, etc.).

```js
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // URL template – replace with your own DEM tiles
  url: 'https://example.com/tiles/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // optional: the size of each tile in pixels (default 256)
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional: the maximum zoom level you have data for
  maxzoom: 14,
  // optional: the DEM’s vertical exaggeration factor (default 1)
  // you can also set this later with the hillshade paint property
  // (see step 4)
});
```

**CORS tip** – The tile server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) so the browser can fetch the DEM tiles.

---

## 3. (Optional) Add a terrain source for 3-D extrusion  

If you also want the map to “fly” over the terrain (e.g. `map.setTerrain()`), add a terrain source that points at the same DEM:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

*Hillshade does **not** require this step; it works purely from the raster-DEM source.*

---

## 4. Add the hillshade layer  

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',          // <-- the raster-DEM source defined above
  // The raster-DEM source can contain multiple font stacks; hillshade ignores them.
  paint: {
    // These are the most common paint properties; tweak to taste.
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315, // sun azimuth (degrees clockwise from north)
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map', // or 'viewport' to keep sun fixed on screen
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,          // vertical exaggeration (default 1)
    'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',
    'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.2)'
  }
});
```

### Paint properties you may want to adjust

| Property | Meaning | Typical range |
|----------|---------|---------------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | Sun azimuth (0 = north) | 0-360 |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | Whether the sun follows the map (`map`) or stays fixed on the viewport (`viewport`) | `"map"` / `"viewport"` |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | Vertical exaggeration of the terrain for shading | 0-5 (1 = true scale) |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | Color of the shadowed side | any CSS color |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | Color of the illuminated side | any CSS color |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | Color of the “mid-tone” side (optional) | any CSS color |

---

## 5. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ raster-DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://example.com/tiles/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14
    });

    // 2️⃣ optional terrain (3-D) – comment out if you don’t need it
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // 3️⃣ hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| No hillshade appears (just the base map) | DEM source not loading (404, CORS) | Open dev-tools → Network → verify DEM tiles are fetched successfully and have `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers. |
| Hillshade looks flat or “washed out” | `hillshade-exaggeration` too low or DEM elevation range is tiny | Increase `hillshade-exaggeration` (e.g., `1.5` or `2`). |
| Tiles load but are black/white | DEM tiles are not 16-bit elevation (e.g., plain RGB imagery) | Use a true elevation raster (GeoTIFF/PNG with elevation encoded as 16-bit or 8-bit signed). |
| The hillshade moves when you rotate the map | `hillshade-illumination-anchor` set to `"viewport"` | Change to `"map"` if you want the sun to stay fixed relative to north. |
| You want the same hillshade on both GL JS and Native | Ensure the DEM source URL is reachable from the native device (no `file://` URLs) and that the same `type: "raster-dem"` is used. | Use an HTTP(S) endpoint that both environments can reach. |

---

### TL;DR code snippet

```js
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.8
  }
});
```

That’s all you need to get a hillshaded terrain visual on top of any MapLibre GL JS style. Happy mapping!

Below is a minimal, copy-and-paste-ready example that shows the three pieces you need:

1. **A `raster-dem` source** – the DEM tiles (usually Mapbox, Terrarium, or any 16-bit elevation tiles).  
2. **A `hillshade` layer** that points at that source.  
3. (Optional) **Terrain** – if you also want 3-D terrain you enable it on the map; it is **not required** for hillshade alone.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map – you can use any raster/vector source you like
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        },

        // 2️⃣ DEM source (raster-dem)
        "dem": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          // Example: Terrarium-encoded tiles from OpenMapTiles
          // Replace with your own tile URL or a Mapbox DEM endpoint.
          tiles: [
            "https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          // The DEM format you are using:
          //   "mapbox"   – Mapbox elevation tiles (encoded as 16-bit signed)
          //   "terrarium" – Terrarium format (16-bit unsigned, offset 32768)
          //   "heightmap" – raw 16-bit PNG (no offset)
          // Choose the one that matches your tiles.
          // For the OpenMapTiles example the format is "terrarium".
          // If you use Mapbox DEMs set "encoding": "mapbox".
          // If you are serving your own PNGs set "encoding": "heightmap".
          // Omit the property if you are using the default (mapbox).
          // encoding: "terrarium"
        }
      },

      // 3️⃣ Base raster layer (optional – just to see the map underneath)
      layers: [
        {
          id: "osm-basemap",
          type: "raster",
          source: "osm"
        },

        // 4️⃣ Hillshade layer
        {
          id: "hillshade",
          type: "hillshade",
          source: "dem",
          // The order matters – put it **after** the basemap but **before**
          // any label or symbol layers you want on top.
          paint: {
            // Default values – tweak to taste
            "hillshade-illumination-direction": 335, // degrees clockwise from north
            "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.5,           // 0 = flat, >1 = more contrast
            "hillshade-shadow-color": "#000000",
            "hillshade-highlight-color": "#ffffff",
            "hillshade-accent-color": "#000000"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // 5️⃣ (Optional) Enable 3-D terrain – not needed for hillshade itself
  // map.setTerrain({ source: "dem", exaggeration: 1.5 });

  // Add navigation controls for convenience
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Piece | Why it’s needed | Key options |
|------|----------------|-------------|
| **`type: "raster-dem"` source** | Supplies elevation data in a format MapLibre can read. | `tiles` (URL template), `tileSize` (usually 256), `encoding` (`"mapbox"`, `"terrarium"` or `"heightmap"`). |
| **`type: "hillshade"` layer** | Renders a shaded relief image from the DEM. | `source` (the DEM source name), `paint` properties to control lighting, contrast, and colors. |
| **`setTerrain` (optional)** | Gives you a 3-D surface you can fly over. Not required for hillshade, but often used together. | `exaggeration` (how much vertical scaling). |

### Common gotchas & how to fix them

| Issue | Symptom | Fix |
|-------|----------|-----|
| **CORS errors** when loading DEM tiles | Console shows `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` blocked. | Serve the tiles with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). If you use a third-party provider, make sure they allow cross-origin requests. |
| **Wrong `encoding`** | Hillshade looks flat or has “banding”. | Verify the DEM format. Terrarium tiles need `encoding: "terrarium"`; Mapbox DEMs need `"mapbox"`; raw 16-bit PNGs need `"heightmap"`. |
| **No hillshade visible** | Layer appears but is completely white/black. | Check that the DEM source actually returns data for the current viewport/zoom. Zoom out to a level where the DEM tiles exist, or inspect the network tab for 404s. |
| **Hillshade covers labels** | Labels disappear under the hillshade. | Place the hillshade layer **below** any symbol/label layers (or reorder with `moveLayer`). |
| **Performance lag** | Map feels sluggish when panning/zooming. | Use a lower `tileSize` (256 is typical) and limit the DEM zoom range (`maxzoom`/`minzoom` in the source). Cache tiles via a CDN if possible. |

### Quick checklist before you go live

1. **DEM source URL** – points to a tile server that returns PNG (or Mapbox) elevation tiles.  
2. **CORS** – headers allow the browser to fetch the tiles.  
3. **`encoding`** matches the tile format.  
4. **Layer order** – hillshade under any vector/symbol layers you need on top.  
5. **Style version** – keep `version: 8` (the only version MapLibre understands).  

That’s it! Drop the snippet into a static HTML file (or integrate the source/layer objects into an existing style JSON) and you’ll see a classic shaded-relief map generated directly from your raster-DEM tiles. Happy mapping!
